.TH DAG 1
.SH NAME
dag, Dag \- draw and edit a graph in dot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dag
.I file
.br
.B Dag
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dag
draws a graph written in
.IR dot (1)'s
language, as
.I dot
lays it out, in a
.IR rio (1)
window.
The
.I file
is the graph's text, a node or an edge to a line:
.IP
.EX
digraph {
	a
	b
	a -> b
}
.EE
.PP
.B "dot -Txdot"
lays the graph out again after each change to the text.
If
.I file
does not exist, the graph starts empty, and
.B write
makes it.
.I Dag
needs the
.B graphviz
package, and adds it with
.IR pkg (1)
if
.B /bin/dot
is not there.
.PP
The selection is a set of lines, those of the selected nodes and edges.
A node's lines are those that name it alone, or, if none does, those of the edges it is in.
.SS Mouse
.TF button\ 3
.TP
button 1
At nothing, adds a node's line before the graph's closing brace,
with the first free name of
.BR n1 ,
.BR n2 ,
.BR n3 ….
Dragged from a node to a node, adds an edge between them,
.B "a -> b"
in a digraph and
.B "a -- b"
in a graph; let go at nothing, it adds a new node too.
A click at a node selects it.
.I Dag
selects what it adds.
.TP
button 2
On a node or an edge, selects it; with Shift, adds it to the selection or takes it out.
Swept over nothing, selects what is inside the box.
A click at nothing selects nothing.
.TP
button 3
A menu:
.B delete
deletes the selected lines, and a node's edges with it;
.B undo
and
.BR redo ;
.B tred
sends the graph through
.IR tred ,
which removes the edges another path implies;
.B write
writes the file;
.B exit
ends
.IR dag .
.PD
.SS Keys
Delete and Backspace delete what is selected, and Escape selects nothing.
Command-Z undoes, Command-Shift-Z redoes, Command-A selects every node and edge,
Command-C copies the selected lines to the snarf buffer, and Command-S writes the file.
.PP
Typing
.BR | ,
.B <
or
.B >
starts a command, shown at the bottom of the window; Return runs it and Escape drops it.
It runs
.IR rc (1)
in the file's directory, as
.IR acme (1)
runs its commands on a selection:
.B |cmd
sends the selected lines, or the whole file if nothing is selected, through
.I cmd
and puts what it prints in their place;
.B <cmd
puts what
.I cmd
prints in place of the selected lines, or before the closing brace;
.B >cmd
sends them to
.IR cmd ,
whose output goes to standard error.
So
.B "|sort -u"
sorts the selected lines, and
.B |tred
reduces the graph.
.SS Dag
.I Dag
is
.I dag
in an
.IR acme (1)
window, whose body is the graph's text.
With no
.IR file ,
it draws the window it runs in,
.BR $winid ;
with one, or outside a window, it opens a new window on the file.
An edit writes only the lines it changes, so the window's
.BR Undo ,
.B Redo
and
.B Put
are Acme's, and
.I Dag
draws the graph again after any change to the text, Undo's too.
Dot is the selection's lines:
.I Dag
selects what dot covers when it starts, and sets dot as the selection changes.
.PP
.I Dag
adds itself to the window's tag.
.B Dag
there ends it, and the window shows its text, to edit as any other.
While the graph shows, a command in the tag beginning with
.BR | ,
.B <
or
.B >
is
.IR dag 's,
as if typed.
The menu has no
.B undo
or
.BR redo ,
and its
.B write
is
.BR Put .
.SH EXAMPLES
In a
.I Dag
window, remove the nodes no edge leaves:
.IP
.EX
|gvpr -c 'N[outdegree==0]{delete(0,$)}'
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/dag
and, for Graphviz,
.BR mk/pkg
and
.B src/graphviz
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR dot (1),
.IR tred (1),
.IR gvpr (1),
.IR pkg (1),
.IR acme (4)
.SH BUGS
Only
.I dot
lays the graph out, and dragging a node does not move it.
.br
.I Dag
draws edges written with a subgraph,
.BR "a -> {b c}" ,
but cannot select them.
.br
It draws text in the window's font, not the graph's.
